body:
Opening a document of a few kilobytes freezes WordCraft for tens of seconds to minutes. For every **distinct** character that no loaded font covers, layout re-reads and parses every system font file from disk. A 2 KB file with 500 Tangut characters froze the desktop app for 45 s here, and the cost grows linearly with the number of distinct characters.

Follow-up to #59: the per-character fallback cache from #233 doesn't help when every character is different.

Reported here at @vangel76's request on Discord.

## Affected

- All releases (0.1.0 to 0.5.0) and `main` at `1d4f741`; the code path exists since `d1e80df`.
- Native builds: desktop app, `wordcraft-cli`, MCP server. Web is not affected (`cfg(not(target_arch = "wasm32"))`).
- Any input format (.txt, .docx, .rtf…), and PDF export.

## Root cause

`FontDb::system_fallback()` (`crates/fonts/src/fontdb.rs:1008`): when no preferred fallback family covers `c`, it walks the whole catalog and, for each file under 40 MB, reads the entire file and parses its charmap:

```rust
// crates/fonts/src/fontdb.rs:1033
for p in paths {
    if std::fs::metadata(&p).map(|m| m.len() > 40 << 20).unwrap_or(true) {
        continue;
    }
    let Ok(data) = std::fs::read(&p) else { continue };
    let hit = enumerate_faces(&data).iter().any(|f| skrifa::FontRef::from_index(&data, f.index).is_ok_and(|f| f.charmap().map(c).is_some()));
    ...
}
self.sys.lock().unwrap_or_else(|e| e.into_inner()).misses.insert(c);
```

The miss is only remembered for that one character, so N distinct uncovered characters cost N full scans of every system font, on the layout thread. Unicode has tens of thousands of assigned characters most systems have no font for (about 6,900 Tangut ideographs alone, more in the CJK extensions).

## Reproduction

```python
#!/usr/bin/env python3
# poc_font_fallback.py N [out.txt]: N distinct Tangut characters (U+17000..)
import sys, unicodedata
n = int(sys.argv[1]) if len(sys.argv) > 1 else 2000
out = sys.argv[2] if len(s…

## Summary

`format.bold` takes `{"value"?: bool}`, so a script can set it on or off. The four Line and Page Breaks commands take only `{}` and toggle:

```
$ wordcraft-cli commands --json
{"id":"format.bold", ..., "params":"{\"value\"?: bool}"}
{"id":"para.keepNext", ..., "params":"{}"}
{"id":"para.keepLines", ..., "params":"{}"}
{"id":"para.pageBreakBefore", ..., "params":"{}"}
{"id":"para.widowControl", ..., "params":"{}"}
```

Paragraph properties carry over to the next paragraph on `text.newParagraph`. A script that wants keep with next on a heading only has to remember the state and toggle it off again on the following paragraph. Our generated document ended up with 47 `<w:keepNext/>` and 113 `<w:keepNext w:val="0"/>`.

## Suggestion

Accept an optional `{"value": bool}` on these four commands, the same as `format.bold`. Without `value` they keep toggling, so the ribbon and existing scripts behave the same as today.

## Summary

Agile encrypted `.docx` packages (ECMA-376 / [MS-OFFCRYPTO] §2.3.4.4) can be changed and still open with the correct password. `EncryptionInfo` is stored in the clear, and decrypt treats `dataIntegrity` as optional. Removing that element turns off the HMAC check. AES-CBC then has no authenticity check, so the decrypted package can differ from what was encrypted.

Confidentiality holds: the password verifier still runs, and a wrong password does not decrypt the package. This is an integrity gap, including for files WordCraft itself writes.

Seen on `main` at `fd0bb2fb1ec081e3eddc4d01917637fef0cafdfe`. No modified file was built and the decrypt path was not executed for this report.

## What the code does

`encrypt` always writes a `dataIntegrity` element ([crates/docx/src/crypt.rs](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/fd0bb2fb1ec081e3eddc4d01917637fef0cafdfe/crates/docx/src/crypt.rs#L162-L178)).

`decrypt` checks the HMAC only when that element is present, then decrypts the package either way:

```rust
if let Some((enc_key, enc_value)) = &info.integrity {
    // ... HMAC ...
    if !same(&info.hash.hmac(hmac_key, &package)?, expected) {
        return Err(bad("the document is damaged (its integrity check failed)"));
    }
}
```

([crates/docx/src/crypt.rs](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/fd0bb2fb1ec081e3eddc4d01917637fef0cafdfe/crates/docx/src/crypt.rs#L95-L106))

The parser maps a missing element to `None` ([same file, lines 655–658](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/fd0bb2fb1ec081e3eddc4d01917637fef0cafdfe/crates/docx/src/crypt.rs#L655-L658)). A hostile-input test renames `<dataIntegrity` away and expects `decrypt` to succeed:

```rust
// Data integrity is optional; without it a good file still opens.
cases.push((with("<dataIntegrity", "<nothing"), package.clone(), false));
```

([same file, lines 958–959](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/fd0bb2fb1ec081e3eddc4d01917637fef0cafdfe/crates/docx/src/crypt.rs#L958-L959)…

Gap #19 (RTF and ODT depth) in docs/gaps.md, export side.

## What happens today

[`odt::export`](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/eae2421/crates/formats/src/odt.rs#L819-L837) first lowers the document to the exchange `Flow` (`model::from_doc_tracked`) and writes ODT from that. `Flow` is the same reduced model the Markdown, HTML and RTF writers use, so everything it doesn't carry is lost on save:

- named paragraph and character styles: the writer emits its own built-in set (default font, Heading 1–6), not the document's styles and their inheritance;
- page setup: one page layout taken from `last_section`, so a document with a portrait body and a landscape section saves as all-landscape;
- headers and footers: none are written, neither first-page nor default;
- anchored text boxes and shapes (a header text box containing a table, for example) are dropped together with their contents;
- fields: PAGE, NUMPAGES, PAGEREF and the TOC are written as their cached text;
- list styles: numbering definitions (levels, formats like `%1.%2.`, indents, restarts) become generic lists;
- table borders, merged cells and rotated cell text.

A quick way to see it: open any .docx with a different first-page header, a landscape section and a numbered heading scheme, save as .odt and open it in LibreOffice.

## Proposal

Write ODT from the full `wordcraft_doc::Document` instead of `Flow`, the way the DOCX writer does. Sketch of the mapping:

| Model | ODF |
|---|---|
| styles with `based_on` | `styles.xml` common styles with `style:parent-style-name`; direct formatting as automatic styles |
| sections | one `style:page-layout` + `style:master-page` per section; the section's first paragraph carries `style:master-page-name`; first-page headers through `style:header-first` |
| headers / footers | `style:header` / `style:footer` (+ `-first`) inside the master page |
| anchored text boxes | `draw:frame` + `draw:text-box`, anchored to page or paragraph |
| numbering | `text:list-st…

## Scope and verification
Read-only source audit of upstream main at `9b4b948c6ecf29401e01ad58626d3c1983b83770`. **Severity:** Medium (editing restrictions). **Confidence:** high — reproduced at runtime against a worktree at this commit, with and without a candidate fix.

## Root cause and evidence
[`crates/engine/src/cmd/tools.rs:469-486`](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/9b4b948c6ecf29401e01ad58626d3c1983b83770/crates/engine/src/cmd/tools.rs#L469-L486) registers `file.versions` as a pure command, and its restore replaces the whole document:

```rust
if let Some(i) = p::u64(v, "restore") {
    let (_, _, d) = s.versions.get(i as usize).cloned().ok_or_else(...)?;
    s.checkpoint("Restore Version");
    s.doc = d;              // the whole document, settings included
    ...
}
```

A pure command never reaches the protection check: [`crates/engine/src/session.rs:1290`](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/9b4b948c6ecf29401e01ad58626d3c1983b83770/crates/engine/src/session.rs#L1290) calls `check_before` only when `spec.mutates`, and the post-check that rolls a change back is likewise gated on it. The snapshot's own `settings.protection`, `settings.protect_hash` and `settings.protect_formatting` therefore replace the live document's.

## Conditions and realistic impact
A snapshot taken before the document was protected restores an unprotected document, and its password hash with it.

Reproduced on a worktree at this commit:

```
s.run("file.versions", &json!({"save": "before"}))?;
s.run("review.restrict", &json!({"mode": "readOnly", "password": "pw"}))?;
// s.doc.settings.protection == Some("readOnly")
s.run("file.versions", &json!({"restore": 0}))?;
// s.doc.settings.protection == None
// s.doc.settings.protect_hash == []   (the password is gone)
```

The command reports success. A script can then edit the document freely, and the document is saved unprotected.

## Recommended remediation
A snapshot's protection state is stale by definition. Restorin…

## Scope and verification
Read-only source audit of upstream main at `9b4b948c6ecf29401e01ad58626d3c1983b83770`. **Severity:** Medium (editing restrictions). **Confidence:** high — reproduced at runtime against a worktree at this commit.

## Root cause and evidence
The Document Inspector's removals delete straight from the document: `file.inspect` with `{"remove": [...]}` calls `remove_hidden(&mut s.doc)` ([`crates/engine/src/cmd/tools.rs:1440`](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/9b4b948c6ecf29401e01ad58626d3c1983b83770/crates/engine/src/cmd/tools.rs#L1440)), and `remove_piece` ([`crates/engine/src/cmd/tools.rs:1561`](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/9b4b948c6ecf29401e01ad58626d3c1983b83770/crates/engine/src/cmd/tools.rs#L1561)) removes whole paragraphs and ranges with `doc.delete_range` and `para.delete`.

Every other deletion in the engine goes through `track_delete` ([`crates/engine/src/cmd/mod.rs:217`](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/9b4b948c6ecf29401e01ad58626d3c1983b83770/crates/engine/src/cmd/mod.rs#L217)) when the document tracks changes, so the deletion leaves a revision behind. The inspector's removals do not.

## Conditions and realistic impact
On a document that tracks changes — which is what `review.restrict {"mode": "trackedChanges"}` turns on — the text goes with no revision to show for it, and the revision trail is broken behind the user's back.

Reproduced on a worktree at this commit:

```
s.run("review.restrict", &json!({"mode": "trackedChanges"}))?;
// s.doc.settings.track_changes == true
let revisions = s.doc.revisions.len();
s.run("file.inspect", &json!({"remove": ["hidden"]}))?;
// s.doc.revisions.len() == revisions   (0 -> 0)
// the hidden text is gone
```

## Recommended remediation
Either track the removals like every other deletion, or refuse them while the document tracks changes, so the trail is never quietly broken.

