Reported from a read-only security and robustness audit (AUDIT.md) of WordCraft at eae2421, re-checked against upstream main [`00bc6a4b`](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/commit/00bc6a4b028013b2dfb0e85e8b326aeed3ecff6d) before filing.

**Severity:** Low (.json input only; pictures and fields shift to the wrong place)
Related: #1337 (closed; its run-length repair landed, the object correspondence it also asked for did not)

**Evidence.** `.json` open repairs runs only ([engine/src/io.rs#L202-L206](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/00bc6a4b028013b2dfb0e85e8b326aeed3ecff6d/crates/engine/src/io.rs#L202-L206)):

```rust
let mut d = serde_json::from_slice::<Document>(bytes)...;
d.normalize_runs();
```

Nothing checks that `objects.len()` equals the number of U+FFFC markers in `text`. Objects are paired with markers by position (`objects_before`), and `Paragraph::delete` drains objects only when the count fits ([doc/src/para.rs#L1186-L1190](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/00bc6a4b028013b2dfb0e85e8b326aeed3ecff6d/crates/doc/src/para.rs#L1186-L1190)):

```rust
if k1 > k0 && k1 <= self.objects.len() {
    self.objects.drain(k0..k1);
}
```

**Impact.** With fewer objects than markers, a delete removes the markers but keeps the objects, so every later object moves to an earlier marker. With more objects than markers, the surplus ones stay in the model unseen (and are counted by every object walk).

**Suggested fix.** In `normalize_runs` (or a `.json`-open pass), drop surplus objects and remove (or pad) unmatched markers. Test: a `.json` paragraph with two markers and three objects (and the reverse) opens with equal counts.

Reported from a read-only security and robustness audit (AUDIT.md) of WordCraft at eae2421, re-checked against upstream main [`00bc6a4b`](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/commit/00bc6a4b028013b2dfb0e85e8b326aeed3ecff6d) before filing.

**Severity:** Low (UI stall of seconds)

**Evidence.** `tidy` repeats a set of `replace` calls until nothing changes ([engine/src/speech.rs#L452-L464](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/00bc6a4b028013b2dfb0e85e8b326aeed3ecff6d/crates/engine/src/speech.rs#L452-L464)):

```rust
loop {
    let before = t.len();
    for (from, to) in [("()", ""), ("[]", ""), ...] {
        t = t.replace(from, to);
    }
    if t.len() == before { break; }
}
```

Each pass removes one level of `((()))`, so n nested pairs cost O(n²). Long sentences are split only at a space (`MAX_SENTENCE`, [#L409-L411](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/00bc6a4b028013b2dfb0e85e8b326aeed3ecff6d/crates/engine/src/speech.rs#L409-L411)), so a paragraph with no spaces reaches `tidy` whole ([#L135-L136](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/00bc6a4b028013b2dfb0e85e8b326aeed3ecff6d/crates/engine/src/speech.rs#L135-L136)).

**Impact.** The audit measured 3.4 s for 40,000 nested pairs, when Read Aloud starts on such a paragraph.

**Suggested fix.** One pass with a stack that drops empty bracket pairs and fixes spacing, plus a forced split at `MAX_SENTENCE` characters when no space is found. Test: 40,000 nested pairs tidy in linear time; a 10,000-character word is split.

Reported from a read-only security and robustness audit (AUDIT.md) of WordCraft at eae2421, re-checked against upstream main [`00bc6a4b`](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/commit/00bc6a4b028013b2dfb0e85e8b326aeed3ecff6d) before filing.

**Severity:** Low (local only)
Related: #1331 (closed; this code is unchanged)

**Evidence.** Every accepted connection gets its own thread, with no limit ([apps/wordcraft/src/control_server.rs#L106-L115](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/00bc6a4b028013b2dfb0e85e8b326aeed3ecff6d/apps/wordcraft/src/control_server.rs#L106-L115)):

```rust
for stream in listener.incoming().flatten() {
    let (tx, ctx, key) = (tx.clone(), ctx.clone(), key.clone());
    std::thread::spawn(move || serve(stream, tx, ctx, &key, first));
}
```

An unauthenticated connection lives until the first-request deadline (`first`, 30 s). `std::thread::spawn` panics when a thread can't be created, so exhausting threads kills the accept loop.

**Impact.** Any local process can open thousands of idle connections, use up threads and memory, and leave the control channel (and MCP `--connect`) dead until the app restarts.

**Suggested fix.** Cap concurrent connections (a counter or a small pool; refuse beyond it), and use `std::thread::Builder::spawn` and drop the connection on error. Test: connections beyond the cap are closed at once and an authenticated client still gets through.

Reported from a read-only security and robustness audit (AUDIT.md) of WordCraft at eae2421, re-checked against upstream main [`00bc6a4b`](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/commit/00bc6a4b028013b2dfb0e85e8b326aeed3ecff6d) before filing.

**Severity:** Low (hygiene; key material left in freed memory)

**Evidence.** The password hash is kept in `Zeroizing`, but each iteration assigns a new `Vec` through it, so the previous one is dropped without being cleared ([docx/src/crypt.rs#L482-L490](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/00bc6a4b028013b2dfb0e85e8b326aeed3ecff6d/crates/docx/src/crypt.rs#L482-L490); the same pattern in `iterate_appended`, [#L386-L393](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/00bc6a4b028013b2dfb0e85e8b326aeed3ecff6d/crates/docx/src/crypt.rs#L386-L393)):

```rust
let mut h = Zeroizing::new(hash.hash(&[salt, &pw]));
for i in 0..spin_count.min(MAX_SPIN_COUNT) {
    *h = hash.hash(&[&i.to_le_bytes(), &h]);
}
```

`Zeroizing` clears only the value it holds when it is dropped. Each replaced buffer is a password-derived intermediate hash, left in freed heap memory. The `aes` 0.9 dependency is built without its `zeroize` feature ([Cargo.toml#L69](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/00bc6a4b028013b2dfb0e85e8b326aeed3ecff6d/Cargo.toml#L69)), so the expanded key schedules in `cbc_decrypt` are not cleared either.

**Suggested fix.** Hash into a fixed buffer and overwrite it in place (`h.copy_from_slice(&next)` and zeroize `next`), or call `zeroize()` on the old value before replacing it; enable `aes`'s `zeroize` feature. Test: none practical beyond a unit test that `iterate` gives the same result after the change.

