## Evidence

`f()` turns any JSON number into `f32`. `ui.resize` forwards it to the viewport without checks:

https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/00bc6a4b028013b2dfb0e85e8b326aeed3ecff6d/crates/ui-egui/src/control.rs#L129
https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/00bc6a4b028013b2dfb0e85e8b326aeed3ecff6d/crates/ui-egui/src/control.rs#L272-L276

```rust
let f = |k: &str| p.get(k).and_then(Value::as_f64).map(|v| v as f32);
// ...
"ui.resize" => {
    let (Some(w), Some(h)) = (f("width"), f("height")) else { return err("missing width/height") };
    ctx.send_viewport_cmd(egui::ViewportCommand::InnerSize(egui::vec2(w, h)));
    ok(json!({}))
}
```

None of these are rejected:
- zero or negative sizes;
- values beyond `f32::MAX`, which become `inf` after `as f32`;
- sizes larger than the GPU's `max_texture_dimension_2d` (commonly 8192 or 16384).

## Conditions and impact

The caller needs the control key (or MCP bridged to the app). A huge size asks winit for a window larger than any surface the GPU can create. On X11 and some Wayland compositors, the request can be granted or only partly clamped. wgpu then gets a surface configuration over its limit, and a wgpu validation error is a panic outside `Session::run`'s guard. That would lose unsaved work in the app. How winit backends handle negative, zero or infinite values is unspecified.

