_Written with an AI assistant at the request of @Yudhistira-Official._

**Version:** seen on WordCraft v0.10.0 (`9130181a`); code checked on `main` at `b70c746f` (2026-10-25).
**Environment:** Linux x86_64, AppImage.

## Problem

With a whole paragraph selected, pressing Tab deletes the paragraph's text and inserts a tab character. Word indents the paragraph instead. This is data loss: the text is only recoverable with Undo.

## Steps to reproduce

1. New document, type `Hello world`.
2. Select the whole paragraph (triple-click, or Ctrl+A in a one-paragraph document).
3. Press Tab.

## Expected (Word)

The paragraph gets a first-line/left indent of one default tab stop (0.5" by default); the text stays.

## Actual

The text is replaced by a tab character. Ctrl+Z restores it.

## Likely cause

`tab()` in `crates/engine/src/cmd/text.rs` (around lines 762–780) only calls `para.indent` when the selection spans different paragraph paths:

```rust
if !s.sel.is_collapsed() && s.sel.anchor.path != s.sel.focus.path {
    // Multiple paragraphs selected: increase indent.
    return super::para::indent(s, &Value::Null);
}
ins_char(s, '\t')
```

A selection inside one paragraph — including a whole-paragraph selection that does not span two paragraph paths — falls through to `ins_char('\t')`, which replaces the selection.

## Suggested implementation

- In `tab()`, detect a whole-paragraph selection:

```rust
let whole_para = !s.sel.is_collapsed()
    && s.sel.anchor.path == s.sel.focus.path
    && {
        let (a, b) = s.sel.ordered();
        a.off == 0 && s.doc.para_at(&a).is_some_and(|p| b.off == p.len())
    };
if !s.sel.is_collapsed() && (s.sel.anchor.path != s.sel.focus.path || whole_para) {
    return super::para::indent(s, &Value::Null);
}
```

- Keep `ins_char('\t')` for a partial selection inside one paragraph, as Word does.
- Consider the case where the selection includes the paragraph mark but ends at `off == len`.
- Add tests in `crates/engine/src/tests_typing.rs`…

On `main` (9003e2b0), `right_to_left_glyphs_stay_where_layout_put_them` (`crates/pdf`) passes on its own but fails when a document with a bulleted list was exported earlier in the same process.

**Reproduce:** add this test to `crates/pdf/src/tests.rs`. It only exports a one-item bulleted list, and its name sorts it first:

```rust
#[test]
fn aaa_export_a_bulleted_list_first() {
    let mut d = Document::new();
    let list = d.numbering.add_list(ListKind::Bullet);
    let mut p = Paragraph::with_text("Item", CharProps::default()).styled("ListParagraph");
    p.props.numbering = Some(NumRef { num: list, level: 0 });
    d.body = vec![para_block(p)];
    export(&d, &PdfOptions::default()).unwrap();
}
```

```
cargo test -p wordcraft-pdf --lib -- --test-threads=1 right_to_left_glyphs_stay              # passes
cargo test -p wordcraft-pdf --lib -- --test-threads=1 right_to_left_glyphs_stay aaa_export   # fails
```

**What changes:** the layout side of the test (`want`) is the same in both runs. The PDF side (`got`) moves: every glyph box of the RTL sample comes out 0.2 pt higher, for example the first one is `[728, 6356, 787, 6433]` alone and `[728, 6358, 787, 6435]` after the bulleted export (tenths of a point, y up). So the PDF written for the same document differs depending on what was exported before it.

**Likely cause (not confirmed):** some state shared across exports, such as a font fallback choice cached when the bullet character is resolved, which then changes a line height or baseline in the later export. If so, this may not be test-only: in the app, exporting a document with bullets and then a right-to-left document could move its text slightly in the PDF.

Seen on Linux (the session's sandbox). Not checked on Windows or macOS, whose fonts differ. I came across it while working on #4257, whose export tests use numbered lists for this reason.

